  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1213 PM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nHeading towards the end of the work week, a warming trend will take \nplace as flow remains primarily out of the west, resulting in \ndownsloping. High temperatures on Thursday and Friday are expected \nto peak in the mid 80s to around 90 degrees east of the Laramie \nRange, with mid 70s to the west. With the gusty, downslope flow and \nminimum RH values of 10-20%, fire weather will again be a concern \ngiven the lack of any moisture, especially as much of our area is \ncurrently in severe to extreme drought. A cold front is expected to \nmove through sometime late Friday into Saturday as lee cyclogenesis \noccurs over the Dakotas. This will drop high temperatures by 5-10 \ndegrees heading into the weekend, with the potential for some more \nactive weather after a dry and breezy week.  \n\nOn Saturday and Sunday, a vigorous closed low is expected to dig \nacross the Pacific Northwest, shifting our mid-level flow to more \nsouthwesterly, thus allowing for another influx of monsoonal \nmoisture. Long range ensemble guidance has PWAT above the 75th \npercentile over Southeastern Wyoming and Western Nebraska during \nthis time, which provides some optimism in terms of beneficial \nmoisture. With the main bulk of the forcing lifting off to the north \nand east over Montana and Dakotas, widespread precipitation isn't \nexpected at this time. Thus, most shower and thunderstorm coverage \nwill likely be highest over the higher terrain and activity over \nlower elevations will be dependent on if these storms can survive \nwith rather poor low-level moisture expected (dewpoints in the low \nto mid 40s). Temperatures will also be cooler over the weekend with \nincreased cloud cover thanks to increasing mid and upper-level \nmoisture and the post-frontal air mass. The flow again becomes more \nzonal on Monday, with a lobe of vorticity moving through the area, \ncontinuing the chance for scattered showers and thunderstorms, \nthough nothing appears to be widespread at this time.",
